---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import NarrativeImage from '../components/NarrativeImage.astro';
import Invitation from '../components/Invitation.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Personal AI setup & support"
  description="Hands-on help setting up AI agents around your own workload. Work with Alastair Brayne on admin, context, planning and workflows you can keep using."
  service="Personal AI agent setup and support"
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap interior-hero">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow red">Personal AI support</p><h1>
          You know AI could help.<br />Let’s get it working<br />for you.
        </h1><p class="lead">
          The admin, the follow-ups, the workstream you keep meaning to sort out. Start with
          something that matters in your day, and get hands-on help putting an agent to work.
        </p><a class="button" href="/enquire/?need=personal"
          >Talk about your workload <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
        >
      </div><div class="personal-paper" aria-hidden="true">
        <p>Still on your mind?</p><ul>
          <li>The inbox</li><li>The follow-ups</li><li>That half-finished plan</li>
        </ul><span>Let’s start with one.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="wrap reading-layout section editorial-layout">
    <nav class="contents" aria-label="On this page">
      <p>On this page</p><a href="#starting">What you bring</a><a href="#setup"
        >What we do together</a
      ><a href="#routine">What you keep using</a><a href="#fit">Who it’s for</a><a href="#questions"
        >Practical questions</a
      >
    </nav>
    <div class="prose editorial-prose">
      <section id="starting">
        <h2>Bring one job you’d like help with.</h2>
        <p class="lead">A real workstream gives us somewhere useful to start.</p>
        <p>
          You might have tried a few AI tools and still find the same jobs waiting for you. Bring an
          example: preparing for meetings, drafting follow-ups, organising project information or
          triaging email. You don’t need to know how to connect the pieces.
        </p>
        <ul class="bring-list">
          <li>The task you want help with</li><li>An example of the information it uses</li><li>
            What you want to check yourself
          </li>
        </ul>
        <NarrativeImage
          name="a-place-to-return"
          alt="Loose notes connect to a notebook, calendar and organised folder."
          caption="Give the work a place you can come back to."
        />
      </section>
      <section id="setup">
        <h2>We build the setup together.</h2>
        <p class="lead">You see how it works, and decide where you stay involved.</p>
        <p>
          We give the agent useful context, connect the tools it needs and try it on your work.
          Claude is one tool I use; the choice depends on what you need and already have.
        </p>
        <div class="service-rows">
          <article>
            <div>
              <h3>Give it context</h3><p class="row-answer">Help it understand the task.</p>
            </div><p>
              Organise relevant information, reusable instructions and sensible places to store
              updates. We agree what the agent needs access to.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <div>
              <h3>Try it on real work</h3><p class="row-answer">
                Learn by working through an example.
              </p>
            </div><p>
              Practise giving direction, checking an answer and adjusting the setup. Agree which
              actions should wait for your approval.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section id="routine" class="routine-chapter">
        <h2>A routine you can return to.</h2>
        <p>
          Clear places for context, ongoing tasks and reusable instructions make it easier to pick
          up the work again. Where scheduled tasks are useful, we agree what they should do and how
          you’ll know whether they worked.
        </p>
        <div class="worked-example">
          <h3>For example: preparing for a meeting.</h3>
          <p class="example-label">An illustrative workflow we could build around your needs.</p>
          <ol class="routine-steps">
            <li>
              <strong>Your context</strong><p>
                Previous notes, the agenda and the questions you need answered.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>The agent’s task</strong><p>
                Bring the relevant points together in a draft briefing.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Your check</strong><p>
                Check the sources, correct the draft and decide what to use.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p class="example-return">
            Next time, return to the same instructions with the new meeting context.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section id="fit">
        <h2>For your workload and your way of working.</h2>
        <p class="lead">You don’t need to be a developer.</p>
        <p>
          I work with business owners, leaders, coaches and people managing their own work.
          Curiosity and a willingness to try things are a useful starting point.
        </p>
        <h3>Room for a busy mind.</h3>
        <p>
          Executive function is one of the meanings behind ExFu. I’m interested in making these
          tools helpful for people with ADHD and other neurodivergent ways of working. We can
          account for how you plan, maintain context and return to a task, without expecting you to
          become a different person.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is practical AI consultancy. My psychotherapy training informs how I work with
          people; these sessions are not therapy or clinical care.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you lead a team, your own experience can also help you decide how to <a
            href="/fractional-support/">bring AI into the wider business</a
          >.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
  <section class="section faq-section" id="questions">
    <div class="wrap reading-layout">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Before we start</p><h2>Tools, costs &amp;<br />one-to-one help.</h2>
      </div><div class="faqs">
        <article class="faq-answer">
          <h3>Do I need to know which tool I want?</h3><p>
            No. Tell me what you would like help with and what you already use. We can work out a
            sensible starting point together.
          </p>
        </article><article class="faq-answer">
          <h3>Will I need a paid AI subscription?</h3><p>
            Some setups need paid tools. We discuss that before agreeing the work, so you understand
            the separate costs and what access is needed.
          </p>
        </article><article class="faq-answer">
          <h3>Is this a course or one-to-one help?</h3><p>
            This offer is hands-on, one-to-one support around your own work. We agree the scope and
            format in conversation.
          </p>
        </article><article class="faq-answer">
          <h3>Can we do this remotely?</h3><p>
            Yes. We can work together remotely. Bristol sessions may also be possible by
            arrangement.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Invitation title="What would you like to get on top of?" />
</Layout>
